    <h2>Common Pitfalls in CEST to EST Conversion</h2>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>1. Confusing CEST (UTC+2) with CET (UTC+1)</h3>
      <p>
        Using winter CET rules (6-hour gap to EST) during summer CEST leads to 1-hour errors in meeting scheduling between Europe and North America.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>2. Overlooking US Daylight Saving Shifts (EDT)</h3>
      <p>
        When North America is on summer time (EDT, UTC-4), the gap to CEST is 6 hours, not 7. Assuming a constant 7-hour gap year-round causes confusion.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>3. Miscalculating Date Rollover for Early European Hours</h3>
      <p>
        European times between 12:00 AM and 6:59 AM CEST map to the previous calendar date in EST. Always double-check calendar dates when booking overnight calls.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>4. Mismanaging the 2-Week Spring/Autumn DST Gap</h3>
      <p>
        The US switches DST on the second Sunday of March, while Europe switches on the last Sunday of March. During this 2-week gap, the offset fluctuates temporarily.
      </p>
    </article>
